  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the bar, Kostya remembers that the sixty-five-thousand-ruble wine bottle was not covered by the entrecôte promotion. Meanwhile, Vika turns off Barinov's television during a football match to demand he fire Max—until a sudden pain makes her clutch her stomach.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Макс, я про вино забыла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Шестьдесят пять тысяч рублей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Минута остаётся до конца матча.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Витя, надо поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, отойди, мне ничего не видно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но больше так никогда не делай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Чего ты хотела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Витя, я хочу поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Нет, я требую.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Чтобы ты его уволил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что с тобой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что-то с ребёнком?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Макс, я про вино забыла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Max, I forgot about the wine.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Max' before landing on 'forgot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Шестьдесят пять тысяч рублей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sixty-five thousand rubles.' In Russian, the idea of 'sixty' flows naturally into 'rubles' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Минута остаётся до конца матча.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'One minute remains until the end of the match.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'remains', turning the action back on the subject. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Витя, надо поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, we need to talk.' Notice how Russian expresses 'proper name' and 'to talk' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, отойди, мне ничего не видно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, step aside, I cannot see anything.' In Russian, the idea of 'Vika' flows naturally into 'one can see' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но больше так никогда не делай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But never do that again.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'but' and finishing on 'do'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Чего ты хотела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you want?' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'wanted'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Витя, я хочу поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, I want to talk.' The grammar packs 'proper name' and 'to talk' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Нет, я требую.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I demand it.' The meaning hinges on 'no' working together with 'demand' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Чтобы ты его уволил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That you fire him.' The grammar packs 'so that' and 'to fire' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что с тобой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is wrong with you?' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'what', while 'you' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что-то с ребёнком?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is something wrong with the baby?' Chef Barinov raises the pitch on 'child' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
